<section class="section-shell questions-section" aria-labelledby="method-heading">
  <div class="section-heading">
    <p class="eyebrow">How Hii reads the world</p>
    <h2 id="method-heading">Different signals require different kinds of trust.</h2>
  </div>
  <div class="disclosure-list">
    <details>
      <summary><span class="disclosure-index" aria-hidden="true">01</span><span>Does a survey tell us what will happen?</span></summary>
      <p>No. It tells us what a defined population reported at a particular time. Public perception can influence behavior, markets, policy, and adaptation, but it is not the same as an outcome.</p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary><span class="disclosure-index" aria-hidden="true">02</span><span>Does an official statement make a claim true?</span></summary>
      <p>No. Government and institutional statements are primary evidence of what an institution said, decided, or did. Their factual claims still require evaluation and may be disputed.</p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary><span class="disclosure-index" aria-hidden="true">03</span><span>Does social-media discussion count as evidence?</span></summary>
      <p>It can be evidence of public discourse, lived experience, emerging claims, and the information environment. Consequential factual claims still need corroboration, and unresolved claims should remain visibly unresolved.</p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary><span class="disclosure-index" aria-hidden="true">04</span><span>Is the World Model trying to predict one future?</span></summary>
      <p>No. It is intended to preserve continuity, expose convergence and contradiction, make uncertainty visible, and help people evaluate possible pathways as conditions change.</p>
    </details>
  </div>
</section>

